diff --git a/docs/chart-history.md b/docs/chart-history.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..faaf8fa
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/chart-history.md
@@ -0,0 +1,57 @@
+# Chart history retention
+
+The collector caches telemetry for the last 2,000 positive heights relative to
+the highest reported height. Advancing heads and out-of-order reports within
+that window are admitted even before the cache fills. An advancing window
+evicts older entries; later backfill cannot restore entries below its floor.
+Genesis remains excluded. This is a disposable dashboard cache, not chain data.
+
+The chart selects the latest 40 available cached heights and then displays them
+in ascending order. It does not fabricate missing heights. Explicit array
+reversal through Lodash's `thru` avoids the incorrect slice/reverse result in
+the pinned Lodash 3.10.1. Ticket arrays retain zero and use null for missing
+counts, preserving their height positions. Dependencies remain unchanged.
+
+Backfill requests contain at most 50 missing heights from the current window,
+newest first in selection and ascending in the transmitted list. `min` and
+`max` match that list's endpoints. Requests stop when the window is complete
+and do not request genesis or return nonfinite endpoints. Backfill can start
+after the first head. History batches receive the same minimal block validation
+as head reports before any cache mutation. They complete their callback once,
+do not reverse the caller's array and do not refresh current report receipts.
+The existing debounced chart callback is the sole history chart broadcast.
+
+Each retained height has at most one propagation record per authenticated node
+ID. When a node changes its reported fork, that reference moves to the new
+fork. Forks with no remaining propagation references are removed, except for
+the selected chart representative. Fork indices are remapped together.
+
+For N distinct node IDs configured at collector startup, retained state is
+bounded by 2,000 heights, at most N propagation records per height and at most
+N + 1 forks per height. Registration is limited to the fixed credential map;
+reconnects reuse the same identity. An insertion can temporarily add one fork
+before pruning. No new numeric fork-limit setting is needed. Changing the
+credential file requires restarting the collector, which resets this cache.
+
+The inherited representative policy remains: first report at a height unless
+an existing trusted source selects another fork. In ordinary configuration the
+trusted IP list is empty; `LITE=true` treats every source as trusted. Neither
+policy proves canonical-chain agreement. The cache is not a complete fork
+archive and does not infer finality, choose a consensus fork or affect mining.
+An incorrect high self-reported height can move this diagnostic window.
+
+Exact duplicates preserve first-seen propagation times. Switching forks gets
+a new receipt and uses the selected fork's first-seen receipt for propagation;
+the collection and cache share an injectable clock. These timing metrics are
+not the separate per-node freshness receipts documented in
+[node-report-freshness.md](node-report-freshness.md).
+
+This bound is not a production memory budget. N and the configured message byte
+limit still determine possible retained size. Slow consumers, outgoing queues,
+connection churn, HTTP/control traffic and the complete telemetry schema need
+separate review. Legacy arrival-based block times and hashrate estimates also
+remain diagnostic; recent reports alone do not establish chain progress.
+
+Run `npm test`, `npm run test:wire` and, with an explicitly disposable PostgreSQL
+cluster configured, `npm run test:persistence`. The chart checks use synthetic
+reports, fake clocks and loopback sockets; they require no chain restore or key.
